  "title": "Homogeneous vector bundles and G-equivariant convolutional neural networks",
  "abstract": "Abstract G -equivariant convolutional neural networks (GCNNs) is a geometric deep learning model for data defined on a homogeneous G -space $$\\mathcal {M}$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mi>M</mml:mi> </mml:math> . GCNNs are designed to respect the global symmetry in $$\\mathcal {M}$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mi>M</mml:mi> </mml:math> , thereby facilitating learning. In this paper, we analyze GCNNs on homogeneous spaces $$\\mathcal {M} = G/K$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mrow> <mml:mi>M</mml:mi> <mml:mo>=</mml:mo> <mml:mi>G</mml:mi> <mml:mo>/</mml:mo> <mml:mi>K</mml:mi> </mml:mrow> </mml:math> in the case of unimodular Lie groups G and compact subgroups $$K \\le G$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mrow> <mml:mi>K</mml:mi> <mml:mo>≤</mml:mo> <mml:mi>G</mml:mi> </mml:mrow> </mml:math> . We demonstrate that homogeneous vector bundles are the natural setting for GCNNs. We also use reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHS) to obtain a sufficient criterion for expressing G -equivariant layers as convolutional layers. Finally, stronger results are obtained for some groups via a connection between RKHS and bandwidth.",
  "year": 2022,
